; Selling a valued lot closes it, whether the sale carries no valuation
; of its own (AAPL: a Value:: tag on the purchase only, $70 for 10
; units) or a different one (VTI: the manual's automated VALUE:: tag
; comes to $5 a unit at the purchase and to $6 at the sale).
;
; A valuation belongs to the lot, which every amount of it shares, and a
; lot keeps the first valuation it is given: each sale names the lot its
; purchase valued, the sale's own valuation is not applied, and print
; shows each sale carrying the purchase's.  3.4.1 closed both lots too,
; but it read the amount a Value:: tag comes to as the value of each
; unit (#2399), so print showed (({$70})) and (({$50})), and it left the
; VTI sale's lot price and date out.

= /^Assets:Fund/
    ; VALUE:: market(amount, post.date, exchange)

2020/01/01 Buy
    Assets:Broker    10 AAPL {$5} [2020/01/01]  ; Value:: $70
    Assets:Cash     $-50

2020/01/01 Buy
    Assets:Fund      10 VTI @ $5
    Assets:Cash

2020/02/01 Sell
    Assets:Broker   -10 AAPL {$5} [2020/01/01] @ $6
    Assets:Cash      $60
    Income:Gains

2020/02/01 Sell
    Assets:Fund     -10 VTI {$5} [2020/01/01] @ $6
    Assets:Cash      $60
    Income:Gains

test bal --lots
                 $20  Assets:Cash
                $-20  Income:Gains
--------------------
                   0
end test

test print
2020/01/01 Buy
    Assets:Broker                       10 AAPL {$5} [2020/01/01] (({$7}))  ; Value:: $70
    Assets:Cash                                 $-50

2020/01/01 Buy
    Assets:Fund                         10 VTI (({$5})) @ $5
    ; VALUE:: market(amount, post.date, exchange)
    Assets:Cash

2020/02/01 Sell
    Assets:Broker                       -10 AAPL {$5} [2020/01/01] (({$7})) @ $6
    Assets:Cash                                  $60
    Income:Gains

2020/02/01 Sell
    Assets:Fund                         -10 VTI {$5} [2020/01/01] (({$5})) @ $6
    ; VALUE:: market(amount, post.date, exchange)
    Assets:Cash                                  $60
    Income:Gains
end test
